      "task": "math_proof_sqrt2",
      "category": "mathematical-reasoning",
      "planning": false,
      "repeat": 1,
      "run_id": "20261030T150917Z-math_proof_sqrt2-r1",
      "repo": "/tmp/eix-agent-eval/20261030T150917Z-math_proof_sqrt2-r1/repo",
      "prompt": "Work autonomously and do not ask questions. Do not change any file. Prove that sqrt(2) is irrational. Use a proof by contradiction and state clearly why the numerator and denominator cannot both be even. End with the line `QED`.",
      "passed": false,
      "verify_exit": null,
      "verify_output": "",
      "send": {
        "exit": 0,
        "stdout": "Assume, for contradiction, that \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is rational.\n\nThen we can write\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2}=\\frac{a}{b},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\), \\(b\\neq 0\\), and the fraction is in lowest terms, so\n\n\\[\n\\gcd(a,b)=1.\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides gives\n\n\\[\n2=\\frac{a^2}{b^2},\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\na^2=2b^2.\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(a^2\\) is even. If \\(a\\) were odd, then \\(a^2\\) would also be odd, so \\(a\\) must be even. Write\n\n\\[\na=2k\n\\]\n\nfor some integer \\(k\\). Substituting,\n\n\\[\n(2k)^2=2b^2\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\n4k^2=2b^2,\n\\]\n\nand therefore\n\n\\[\nb^2=2k^2.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b^2\\) is even, which again implies that \\(b\\) is even. Hence both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by \\(2\\).\n\nThis contradicts \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\): if both numerator and denominator are even, then \\(\\frac{a}{b}\\) is not in lowest terms, since both can be divided by \\(2\\). Equivalently, the assumption that a rational number can be written with an even numerator and an even denominator in lowest terms contradicts the definition of lowest terms itself.\n\nTherefore no such representation exists, and \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational.\n\nQED\n",
        "stderr": "",
