    {
      "task": "math_proof_inf_primes",
      "category": "mathematical-reasoning",
      "planning": false,
      "repeat": 1,
      "run_id": "20261030T150921Z-math_proof_inf_primes-r1",
      "repo": "/tmp/eix-agent-eval/20261030T150921Z-math_proof_inf_primes-r1/repo",
      "prompt": "Work autonomously and do not ask questions. Do not change any file. Prove by contradiction that there are infinitely many primes, using the product of all primes plus one. Explain why the new number must have a prime divisor not in the list. End with the line `QED`.",
      "passed": true,
      "verify_exit": null,
      "verify_output": "",
      "send": {
        "exit": 0,
        "stdout": "**Proof by contradiction.**\n\nAssume there are only finitely many primes, and list them all:\n\n\\[\np_1, p_2, \\dots, p_n.\n\\]\n\nForm the number\n\n\\[\nN = p_1p_2\\cdots p_n + 1.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(N \\ge 2+1 = 3\\), we have \\(N>1\\). Every integer greater than \\(1\\) has at least one prime divisor, so let \\(q\\) be a prime divisor of \\(N\\).\n\n**Claim: \\(q\\) is not among \\(p_1,\\dots,p_n\\).**\n\nSuppose instead that \\(q = p_i\\) for some \\(i\\). Then \\(q\\) divides the product \\(p_1p_2\\cdots p_n\\), and by assumption \\(q\\) also divides \\(N\\). Hence \\(q\\) divides the difference\n\n\\[\nN - p_1p_2\\cdots p_n = 1.\n\\]\n\nBut no prime divides \\(1\\), a contradiction. Therefore \\(q \\notin \\{p_1,\\dots,p_n\\}\\).\n\nSo \\(q\\) is a prime not in the list, contradicting the assumption that the list contained *all* primes. (Note that \\(N\\) may itself be prime, in which case \\(q=N\\); otherwise \\(q\\) is a proper prime factor of \\(N\\). Either way, \\(q\\) is a prime outside the list.)\n\nThe assumption of finitely many primes is therefore false, so there are infinitely many primes.\n\nQED\n",
        "stderr": "",
        "timed_out": false,
        "duration_s": 6.717
      },
