    {
      "task": "math_proof_induction_odds",
      "category": "mathematical-reasoning",
      "planning": false,
      "repeat": 1,
      "run_id": "20261030T150928Z-math_proof_induction_odds-r1",
      "repo": "/tmp/eix-agent-eval/20261030T150928Z-math_proof_induction_odds-r1/repo",
      "prompt": "Work autonomously and do not ask questions. Do not change any file. Prove by induction that 1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n - 1) = n^2 for every integer n >= 1. Label the base case and the inductive step explicitly. End with the line `QED`.",
      "passed": true,
      "verify_exit": null,
      "verify_output": "",
      "send": {
        "exit": 0,
        "stdout": "**Claim.** For every integer \\(n \\ge 1\\),\n\\[\n1+3+5+\\cdots+(2n-1)=n^2.\n\\]\n\n**Base case.** Let \\(n=1\\). The left side is \\(1\\), and the right side is \\(1^2=1\\). Hence the claim holds for \\(n=1\\).\n\n**Inductive step.** Assume the claim holds for some integer \\(k \\ge 1\\), so that\n\\[\n1+3+5+\\cdots+(2k-1)=k^2.\n\\]\nWe must show the claim holds for \\(k+1\\). Adding the next odd integer, \\(2(k+1)-1=2k+1\\), to both sides gives\n\\[\n1+3+5+\\cdots+(2k-1)+(2(k+1)-1)\n= k^2+(2k+1)\n= (k+1)^2.\n\\]\nThus, if the claim holds for \\(k\\), it also holds for \\(k+1\\).\n\nBy the principle of mathematical induction, the claim holds for every integer \\(n \\ge 1\\).\n\nQED\n",
        "stderr": "",
        "timed_out": false,
        "duration_s": 6.008
      },
