    {
      "task": "math_def_limit",
      "category": "mathematical-reasoning",
      "planning": false,
      "repeat": 1,
      "run_id": "20261030T150934Z-math_def_limit-r1",
      "repo": "/tmp/eix-agent-eval/20261030T150934Z-math_def_limit-r1/repo",
      "prompt": "Work autonomously and do not ask questions. Do not change any file. State the epsilon-delta definition of lim_{x -> a} f(x) = L, then use it to prove that lim_{x -> 2} (3x + 1) = 7. Give an explicit choice of delta in terms of epsilon. End with the line `QED`.",
      "passed": true,
      "verify_exit": null,
      "verify_output": "",
      "send": {
        "exit": 0,
        "stdout": "**Definition.** Let \\(f\\) be defined on some punctured neighborhood of \\(a\\). Then \\(\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L\\) means: for every \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\) there exists \\(\\delta > 0\\) such that for all \\(x\\),\n\n\\[\n0 < |x - a| < \\delta \\implies |f(x) - L| < \\varepsilon .\n\\]\n\n**Claim.** \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x+1) = 7\\).\n\n**Proof.** Let \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\) be given and set\n\n\\[\n\\delta = \\frac{\\varepsilon}{3} > 0 .\n\\]\n\nTake any \\(x\\) with \\(0 < |x - 2| < \\delta\\). Then\n\n\\[\n|f(x) - L| = |(3x+1) - 7| = |3x - 6| = 3|x - 2| < 3\\delta = 3\\cdot\\frac{\\varepsilon}{3} = \\varepsilon .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\) was arbitrary, for every \\(\\varepsilon > 0\\) the choice \\(\\delta = \\varepsilon/3\\) satisfies the definition. Hence \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2}(3x+1) = 7\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\nQED\n",
        "stderr": "",
        "timed_out": false,
        "duration_s": 4.305
