      "repo": "/tmp/eix-agent-eval/20261030T150938Z-math_def_continuity-r1/repo",
      "prompt": "Work autonomously and do not ask questions. Do not change any file. Give the epsilon-delta definition of continuity of f at a point a, then prove that f(x) = x^2 is continuous at a = 3. End with the line `QED`.",
      "passed": true,
      "verify_exit": null,
      "verify_output": "",
      "send": {
        "exit": 0,
        "stdout": "Let \\(f\\) be defined on some domain containing \\(a\\). We say \\(f\\) is continuous at \\(a\\) if for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there exists \\(\\delta>0\\) such that for all \\(x\\) in the domain of \\(f\\),\n\n\\[\n|x-a|<\\delta \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad |f(x)-f(a)|<\\varepsilon.\n\\]\n\n**Claim.** \\(f(x)=x^2\\) is continuous at \\(a=3\\).\n\nLet \\(\\varepsilon>0\\). We want \\(|x^2-9|<\\varepsilon\\) whenever \\(|x-3|<\\delta\\). Factor:\n\n\\[\n|x^2-9|=|x-3||x+3|.\n\\]\n\nRestrict \\(\\delta\\le 1\\). Then \\(|x-3|<1\\) implies \\(2<x<4\\), so\n\n\\[\n|x+3|=x+3<7.\n\\]\n\nChoose\n\n\\[\n\\delta=\\min\\left(1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{7}\\right)>0.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|x-3|<\\delta\\), then \\(|x+3|<7\\), and therefore\n\n\\[\n|x^2-9|=|x-3||x+3|\n< \\frac{\\varepsilon}{7}\\cdot 7\n=\\varepsilon.\n\\]\n\nHence for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) there exists \\(\\delta>0\\) such that \\(|x-3|<\\delta\\) implies \\(|x^2-9|<\\varepsilon\\). Therefore \\(f(x)=x^2\\) is continuous at \\(a=3\\).\n\nQED\n",
        "stderr": "",
        "timed_out": false,
        "duration_s": 5.09
      },
