        "stdout": "Assume, for contradiction, that \\([0,1]\\) is countable. Then\n\n\\[\n[0,1]=\\{x_1,x_2,x_3,\\dots\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite each number using the decimal expansion that does not eventually consist only of 9s. Thus, for example, \\(1=1.000\\ldots\\), not \\(0.999\\ldots\\). Write\n\n\\[\nx_n=0.d_{n1}d_{n2}d_{n3}\\cdots,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d_{nj}\\in\\{0,\\dots,9\\}\\) is the \\(j\\)-th digit after the decimal point. (If \\(x_n=1\\), its digits after the decimal point are all \\(0\\).)\n\nNow construct a new number \\(y=0.e_1e_2e_3\\cdots\\) by defining its \\(n\\)-th digit as\n\n\\[\ne_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & d_{nn}\\neq 1,\\\\\n2, & d_{nn}=1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(y\\in[0,1]\\), since it lies between \\(0.111\\ldots\\) and \\(0.222\\ldots\\).\n\nFor every \\(n\\), the number \\(y\\) differs from \\(x_n\\) in the \\(n\\)-th decimal place: \\(e_n\\neq d_{nn}\\). Moreover, the expansion of \\(y\\) uses only the digits \\(1\\) and \\(2\\), so it never ends in an infinite string of 9s. Therefore it has the unique decimal expansion not ending in all 9s, and equality \\(y=x_n\\) would force their decimal digits to agree everywhere. This is impossible because their \\(n\\)-th digits differ.\n\nHence \\(y\\) is a real number in \\([0,1]\\) that is not equal to any \\(x_n\\). This contradicts the assumption that \\(x_1,x_2,\\dots\\) lists every element of \\([0,1]\\). Therefore \\([0,1]\\) is uncountable.\n\nQED\n",
